Z||000000||Richard Parnell||05-20-2000||04:37 PM||||Bench bled new master.  Installed new slave. Installed master.  Removed bleeder from slave and allowed fluid to pass through for an hour or so-no air bubbles. Had back end of car 6-8 inches in air. Pulled slave rod back into cylinder-removed all air.  Reinstalled bleeder with rod still pulled into cylinder.  Strarted engine still wouldn't go into gear.  Clutch pedal (new steel) feels solid when depressing.  Turned engine off put car in first started engine-cars moves forward but not as bad as before-still cannot change gears.  Had wife (who hates car) push in clutch pedal.  Watched slave push clutch lever arm seemed to move a good distance.  Had wife push pedal in 3/4 of way released bleeder.  Did this 4-5 times, releasing bleeder slightly each time- no air.  Tried clutch again.  Still can't shift.  It seems to be right on the edge of working.  I need another 1/8-1/4 inch and it will work but how to get that.  I do see the bracket that slave is attached to bend just slightly when cluth pedal is depressed.But bracket looks solid-no cracks.  Ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||05-20-2000||05:07 PM||||Some fictional person had a quote about impossible and merely improbable.  "bend just slightly" seems unlikely as the culprit, but what is left? Too big a slave? Too small a master? Wrong pressure plate?<P>Shouldn't be any visible "give" in the system at all.<P>Then again, Fiero clutch function depends on the phase of the moon during the slave's conception.||||reg||12
Z||000002||bHooper||05-20-2000||05:16 PM||||Richard, I may have asked this b4... are there any spacers on the bolts holding the slave to the bracket?  if so are these spacers between the slave and the bracket?  if these spacers do exist, try removeing them , reinstall the slave without them, and give it a try.<P>my car had the spacers, and would not go into reverse!  it would go into 1st and 2nd fine, but not reverse.<P>let me know.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||05-20-2000||05:21 PM||||The slave is a new one I got from George at the Fiero Shop (got it right this time George) and a new steel pedal.  It (the slave is cast but the same length as original. The masters I've bought are are identical to the one I removed.  I really don't know where to go next.  Except find a helper (the wife split for some opera or something) that is willing to work the clutch for me for a few hours.  I guess I'll do the old pump the clutch release the bleeder routine until it works or my helper leaves.  The gravity feed doesn't seem to be doing it.  One note if I pump the clutch a whole bunch I do see some very small (like a mist) air bubbles coming back up into the reservoir of the master.  But I do not see any air coming from the bleeder.  I'm so close to getting it working, I just know it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||05-20-2000||05:23 PM||||No spacers between bracket and slave.  I am thinking of reinstalling the slave to the passenger side of the bracket even though it's suppsed to go on the drivers side of the bracket. Maybe that will give me the fraction of an inch I need.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||05-20-2000||05:54 PM||||<BR>Richard, it's a little confusing following your clutch problems and what you've gone through because of the numerous threads, but Phaeton has touched upon a good point. The slave can come in two diameters, 13/16 and 15/16 of an inch. The <I>smaller</I> diameter unit will disengage the clutch more than the larger one. If there are no other reasons for your problems, I'd suggest making sure that you are using a 13/16 inch unit.<P>As has been mentioned elsewhere, moving the slave closer to the clutch arm will make NO difference. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||beachbomb||05-20-2000||05:57 PM||||i bought my '84 indy a month ago, and it had almost same prob, would not go into reverse and almost would not go into 1st or 2nd, only went into reverse when car was shut off, if car was in first with clutch pushed in and then tryed to start it, car would move forward slightly, anyway--- i took it to a transmission shop and they said there was two bolts missing from trans and two others loose, repaired that and it works great now! Just my 2 cents, you might want to check that..jim||||reg||4
Z||000007||William Federle||05-21-2000||08:16 AM||||Richard,<BR>      During my fight with the slave cylinder I read somewhere, maybe an Archism,<BR>that there should be 1-1/4" of travel. Before the new pedal and bleeding and master cylinder, it was 3/4" max. After (and now) it's about 1-1/8". If you have 1-1/8" or more of travel then whatever's wrong, it's not the clutch pedal or associated hydraulics. The thing the clutch pedal pushes on is clamped onto a shaft that it rotates down in the transmission. If the travel is OK then I'd think its time to look at the alignment like you said or at the thing it pushes on.<P>Hang in there. If you're eliminating possibilities, you're making progress. ||||reg||1
